 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 17,
   "y": 20,
   "text": "A CUTAWAY BOG. Across the Midlands, Bord na M\u00f3na cut peat by machine for power stations and for gardens through most of the 1900s. When the peat is gone, bare ground called cutaway is left behind. Burning peat for electricity ended in 2020, and many cutaways are now being left to re-wet and grow wetland, scrub and woodland.",
   "plaque": "lease_clonbeg_cutaway",
   "title": "A Cutaway Bog"
  },
  {
   "x": 13,
   "y": 25,
   "text": "THE OLDEST FIRE. At Lough Boora in County Offaly, workers cutting a bog found flint tools and traces of fires left by hunter-gatherers more than 8,000 years ago, the Mesolithic people. A bog keeps what falls into it, so what the machines uncovered was a landscape no one had seen since.",
   "plaque": "lease_clonbeg_mesolithic",
   "title": "The Oldest Fire"
  },
  {
   "x": 17,
   "y": 26,
   "text": "TURF AND THE SLE\u00c1N. For centuries families cut their own fuel by hand with a sle\u00e1n, a narrow spade with a wing on one side, in spring. The sods were turned, footed, and stacked to dry in summer. Many still do, though bogs now belong to a wider story about carbon and wildlife.",
   "plaque": "lease_clonbeg_turf",
   "title": "Turf and the Sle\u00e1n"
  }
 ],
 "spawn": [
